 {
  "case_id": 4417931,
  "cite": "5 Tex. Ct. App. 89",
  "court": "Texas Courts of Appeals",
  "year": 1878,
  "jurisdiction": "Tex.",
  "relevant": true,
  "relevance_score": 0.35,
  "polarity": "favorable",
  "who_was_letting": "householder",
  "duration_of_occupancy": "unclear",
  "characterization": "lease",
  "holding_summary": "The owner of a house who leases it to a tenant, knowing the tenant intends to use it for public prostitution, is not thereby guilty of keeping a disorderly house under Texas's penal statute, contrary to the common-law rule that such letting was itself indictable. The court reasoned that the Texas Penal Code displaces the common law and does not expressly criminalize the mere act of leasing.",
  "doctrinal_concepts": [],
  "new_terms_observed": [],
  "quotes": [
   {
    "text": "The owner of a house is not guilty of an oifence under the laws of this State for leasing his house to a tenant, knowing that it is to be kept for the purpose of public prostitution.",
    "supports": "holding_summary",
    "status": "verified",
    "raw_span": [
     64,
     246
    ],
    "reporter_page": "89"
   },
   {
    "text": "the letting of a house is in itself an innocent act, but the defendant let his house for the purposes of prostitution, and he knew that it was used accordingly",
    "supports": "characterization",
    "status": "verified",
    "raw_span": [
     4305,
     4464
    ],
    "reporter_page": "91"
   },
   {
    "text": "At common law, it seems clear that such letting or hiring, with a guilty knowledge, would make the landlord indictable as a principal in keeping the house.",
    "supports": "polarity",
    "status": "verified",
    "raw_span": [
     4591,
     4746
    ],
    "reporter_page": "91"
   }
  ],
  "worker": "claude",
  "batch_id": "cycle-003-shard-01-batch-007",
  "notes": "Not a lodging/boarding-house case on its facts (disorderly house/prostitution, ordinary tenancy of unspecified duration), but doctrinally useful dicta: 'the letting of a house is in itself an innocent act,' i.e. letting treated as an ordinary, non-criminal incident of ownership. Marked favorable for that reason despite the adverse underlying use; low relevance_score reflects the off-topic fact pattern.",
  "extraction_status": "ok"
 },
